// #188 (PERF-CLOSURE-NATIVE) reproducer, committed per R15.
//
// The third first-class closure call shape: a capturing closure RETURNED from a
// function and then called.
//
// Measured at e12c82d2 this shape produced `program_fallback:
// w17-marshal-residual` — a module-binding misclassification that masked
// everything behind it. Slice 1 removed that and the real blocker became
// visible: `mk` had no compile-time-proven `FrameDescriptor.return_kind`,
// because a declared function-type return annotation fell through
// `classify_type_annotation_metadata` to `FrameReturnMetadata::unknown()`, so
// the JIT's W36 named-function callgraph refused the whole program.
//
// Stage 3 stamps it — but on a PROVEN carrier, never on the annotation alone.
// A `(int) => int` slot can hold either an `Arc<HeapValue::ClosureRaw>` or the
// JIT-internal NaN-boxed `box_function(fn_id)` a NAMED function reference
// lowers to, and a slot stamped `Ptr(HeapKind::Closure)` is Arc-decremented
// unconditionally by `arc_closure_release`. Stamping the wrong one is a
// use-after-free, not a mis-optimization.
//
// `mk` qualifies because its body is exactly `return <closure literal>`, which
// makes the compiler set `emit_make_closure_heap_next` and emit
// `ClosureAlloc { escapes: true }` — the heap Arc carrier on both tiers. See
// `body_provably_returns_heap_closure` in `shape-vm compiler/helpers.rs`;
// `fn pick() -> (int) => int { return some_named_fn }` is NOT stamped and keeps
// this file's former bail.
//
// Expected now: no program fallback; `mk` and `__closure_0` both
// `native-dispatched`, the closure body entered 200 times with zero
// interpreter dispatches. The file stays a VM==JIT anchor either way.


fn mk(k: int) -> (int) => int { return |x: int| x * k }

let f = mk(3)
let mut total = 0
for i in 0..200 {
    total = total + f(i)
}
print(total)
